<style is:global>@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Free";font-style:normal;font-weight:900;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-solid-900.woff2) format('woff2')}@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Brands";font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-brands-400.woff2) format('woff2')}@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Free";font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-regular-400.woff2) format('woff2')}</style>
<section class="relative min-h-[55vh] flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden bg-[#030a16]"><div class="absolute inset-0 z-0"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782368169/eurocert/m10.png.png" class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-30" alt="ISO 22301 Business Continuity Management"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-[#030a16]/70 via-[#030a16]/40 to-[#030a16]"></div></div><div class="container mx-auto px-6 relative z-10 text-center py-20"><div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-eurogold-400/30 bg-eurogold-400/5 mb-6"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-eurogold-400 animate-pulse"></span><span class="heading-font text-[10px] text-eurogold-400 tracking-[0.3em] uppercase font-bold">Business continuity management</span></div><h1 class="heading-font text-4xl sm:text-6xl md:text-7xl font-bold text-white mb-3 tracking-tight" style="text-shadow:0 0 30px rgba(212,168,67,0.3)">ISO 22301  -  Business Continuity Management</h1><p class="text-white/90 text-sm md:text-base max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">Framework for preparing, responding to, and recovering from disruptive incidents.</p></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="grid lg:grid-cols-5 gap-10 items-start"><div class="lg:col-span-3 space-y-6"><div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Standard Overview</h4><h2 class="heading-font text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">ISO 22301  -  <span class="text-euro-600">Business Continuity Management</span></h2></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-[15px] leading-relaxed">To assess applicability, define organizational boundaries, the standard edition, services or activities, locations and intended assurance route before setting an audit scope.</p><div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-euro-100 bg-euro-50/40"><div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3"><div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl bg-euro-600 flex items-center justify-center"><i class="fas fa-info-circle text-white text-xs"></i></div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">What is ISO 22301?</h4></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">ISO 22301 specifies requirements for a business continuity management system (BCMS). It provides a framework for preparing for disruption, responding in a coordinated way and recovering prioritized activities within defined objectives. It does not predict every incident or guarantee uninterrupted operations. The system should reflect organizational context, obligations, dependencies and the consequences of disruption.</p></div></div><div class="lg:col-span-2 relative rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-slate-100 h-[320px] md:h-[400px] group"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782368169/eurocert/m10.png.png" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-700 group-hover:scale-105" alt="ISO 22301 Business Continuity Management"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-slate-900/20 via-transparent to-transparent"></div></div></div></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-[#f8fafc]"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="text-center mb-10"><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Discuss the standard, business continuity scope and applicable audit criteria for your organisation.</p><div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 justify-center"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#030a16] bg-eurogold-400 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-eurogold-300 transition-all shadow-lg shadow-eurogold-400/20" href="/contact/">Get Started <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-[9px]"></i></a><a href="tel:+919316012883" onclick="return gtag_report_conversion('tel:+919316012883');" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-white border border-white/15 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-white/5 transition-all"><i class="fas fa-phone text-[9px]"></i> Call Us Now</a></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white" aria-labelledby="standard-details"><div class="container mx-auto px-6 max-w-5xl space-y-8"><h2 id="standard-details" class="heading-font text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Applying ISO 22301 business continuity management in practice</h2><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Who should consider it?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Organisations of any size or sector can consider ISO 22301 when interruptions to people, facilities, technology, suppliers or critical processes could affect customers or obligations. It is relevant to financial and professional services, manufacturing, healthcare, logistics, utilities, public services and time-sensitive operations. Set the scope around meaningful activities, sites, services and dependencies. Include outsourced services and suppliers in the analysis where their failure could cause disruption, while recognizing that third-party controls may require contractual coordination.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Key requirements</h3><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed"><li>Analyze organizational context, interested parties and obligations; establish BCMS leadership, policy, responsibilities, objectives and controlled documented information.</li><li>Identify activities, dependencies and impacts through business impact analysis, then assess disruption risks and prioritize recovery requirements. Keep the analysis current as services change.</li><li>Develop continuity strategies, response, communication, resource, recovery and supplier arrangements, with roles and escalation paths.</li><li>Establish plans and procedures for relevant disruption scenarios and recovery priorities; provide competence and awareness for those who may need to act.</li><li>Exercise arrangements, evaluate results, monitor performance, learn from incidents, conduct internal audits and management reviews, and correct weaknesses.</li></ul></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Certification and audit lifecycle</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Stage 1 reviews the BCMS scope, context, impact-analysis approach, risk assessment, continuity arrangements and readiness for the main audit. Stage 2 tests implementation and effectiveness by sampling plans, response roles, exercise results, recovery evidence, supplier dependencies, communication arrangements and staff awareness. Nonconformities follow the scheme's corrective-action process before a certification decision. Surveillance audits assess continuing operation at planned intervals; recertification is considered before the cycle ends. An audit samples the system; it does not predict that every contingency will succeed. Timing depends on complexity, sites, critical activities, shifts, dependencies, outsourcing and system maturity. No fixed preparation or certification duration can be assumed.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Documents to prepare</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Prepare the BCMS scope, context and obligations; business impact analyses and risk assessments; prioritized activities and recovery objectives; continuity strategies; response and recovery plans; emergency and crisis communications; roles and contact arrangements; supplier and dependency assessments; exercise schedules, scenarios and results; incident records; internal audits; management reviews; and corrective actions. Protect sensitive security, contact and recovery information in audit samples.</p></div><div><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">A continuity exercise should have a stated objective, participants, scenario assumptions, evaluation method and recorded lessons. Organizations should distinguish a tabletop discussion from a technical recovery test and select exercise types that are safe and meaningful for the scope. Findings should lead to assigned actions and follow-up checks. Plans need controlled versions, accessible contact information and clear triggers for activation; test records help show whether assumptions, dependencies and recovery priorities remain realistic.</p><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Frequently asked questions</h3><div class="space-y-4"><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Does ISO 22301 guarantee business continuity?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. It requires systematic preparedness, response and recovery but cannot eliminate disruption or guarantee a particular outcome.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Is the standard suitable for small organisations?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">It can be applied by organisations of different sizes. Arrangements should be proportionate to activities, risks and obligations.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">What is a business impact analysis?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">It identifies important activities and consequences of disruption over time, helping inform priorities and recovery arrangements; it should be evidence-based and reviewed.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">What evidence is checked at stage 2?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Auditors may sample continuity plans, exercises, incident learning, dependency management, response roles, communications and staff awareness.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">When are surveillance and recertification needed?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Where certification is granted, surveillance and recertification follow the planned cycle and rules of the applicable scheme.</p></article></div></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
